GAD.reg("lesson","cma.oh.classify",{"id":"cma.oh.classify","title":"Classifying overheads, allocation and apportionment","md":"## In plain words\n\n### The ₹15.78 lakh heap called \"miscellaneous\"\n\nTapri's samosa factory in Indore spent ₹15.78 lakh last month on things that are not potatoes, flour or the wages of the people shaping samosas.\n\n> **Kesar:** Rent, gas, the supervisor, the canteen... put it all under \"miscellaneous\". Sab adjust ho jayega.\n> **Ritu:** Per unit, please. How much of this heap belongs to one pack of samosas? \"Miscellaneous\" cannot tell me.\n\nThe potatoes in a pack can be weighed and charged to that pack. The factory rent cannot: no pack \"uses\" ₹3 of rent that anyone can point to. Costs like the rent, the supervisor's salary and the fryer lubricant are **overheads**: costs that cannot be conveniently traced to any one unit of output, because they are incurred for output in general.\n\nRitu sorts the heap twice.\n\nFirst, **by kind**: what the cost is for (the factory, the office, selling or delivery), how it moves with output (fixed, variable or in between), what it is made of (indirect material, indirect employee cost, indirect expenses) and whether a manager can control it.\n\nSecond, **by place**: she gives each cost to a department. A cost that belongs wholly to one department goes there in full: the gas read from the Frying department's own meter is **allocated** to Frying. A cost that several departments share is split in fair proportions: the factory rent is **apportioned** by the floor area each department occupies. Then the costs of the departments that only help (Stores, Maintenance) are passed on to the departments that make the product (**re-apportionment**), and finally each production department's total is charged to the packs it makes (**absorption**).\n\n::: analogy The flat-share bills\nFour friends share a flat. Each one's mobile bill is that person's alone: allocation. The rent is split by the size of each room, and the cook's salary by how many meals each eats: apportionment, each on its own fair basis. A bill nobody can trace gets split anyway, because someone has to pay it; the only question is how fairly.\n:::\n\n::: key\nA cost is **allocated** when the whole of it belongs to one cost centre, and **apportioned** when it is shared and must be split on a fair basis. The link between the expense and the cost centre decides which, not the kind of expense: power on its own meter is allocated, power on a common meter is apportioned.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### What counts as an overhead\n\nOverheads are the expenditure which cannot be conveniently traced to or identified with any particular cost unit; they are incurred for output generally and not for a particular work order, e.g. wages of watch and ward staff, heating and lighting of the factory (SM 4.2). They are the aggregate of **indirect material**, **indirect employee cost** and **indirect expenses** (SM 1.26), and they arise in the factory, in administration and in selling and distribution. In a manufacturing concern they often exceed direct wages or direct materials, and at times both together, so ignoring them is \"a grave mistake\" (SM 4.2).\n\n### Four ways to sort the heap\n\nICAI classifies overheads four ways, in this order (SM 4.3–4.5):\n\n::: diagram The four classifications of overheads\n{\"type\": \"layers\", \"layers\": [{\"label\": \"By function\", \"note\": \"factory, administration, selling and distribution\", \"right\": \"what it is for\"}, {\"label\": \"By nature\", \"note\": \"fixed, variable, semi-variable\", \"right\": \"how it moves with output\"}, {\"label\": \"By element\", \"note\": \"indirect material, employee cost, expenses\", \"right\": \"what it is made of\"}, {\"label\": \"By control\", \"note\": \"controllable, uncontrollable\", \"right\": \"who can influence it\"}], \"caption\": \"One cost sits in all four at once: factory rent is a factory overhead, fixed, an indirect expense and uncontrollable by the shop-floor manager.\"}\n:::\n\n**By function**\n\n| Class | ICAI's description | ICAI's examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Factory (manufacturing, production) | Indirect cost of manufacturing, from procurement of materials to completion of the finished product | Stock keeping, repairs of plant, depreciation of factory building, indirect labour, cost of primary packing, insurance of plant |\n| Office and administrative | General management and administration, not related directly to production, selling, distribution or R&D | Office salaries, office repairs and depreciation, postage and stationery, lease rental on operating lease, accounts and audit |\n| Selling and distribution | Selling: sale of products and sales management. Distribution: making the product available for sale in the market | Salesmen's commission, advertisement, sales office; delivery vans, transit insurance, warehouse and cold storage, secondary packing |\n\n**By nature**\n\n| Class | ICAI's description | ICAI's examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fixed | Incurred for a period; within output and turnover limits, unaffected by changes in activity | Salary of permanent employees, depreciation, interest on capital, insurance |\n| Variable | Vary with the volume of activity | Indirect materials, power and fuel, lubricants, tools and spares |\n| Semi-variable | Contain fixed and variable components | Electricity, water, telephone and internet |\n\n**By element**\n\n| Class | ICAI's description | ICAI's examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Indirect materials | Do not normally form part of the finished product | Lubricants, cotton waste, stores of service departments |\n| Indirect employee cost | Cannot be allocated but can be apportioned or absorbed | Foreman's and supervisor's salary, administration staff |\n| Indirect expenses | Cannot be directly, conveniently and wholly allocated to cost centres | Rates and taxes, insurance, depreciation, advertisement |\n\n**By control**\n\n| Class | ICAI's description | ICAI's examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Controllable | Can be controlled by appropriate managerial influence and policies | Materials, wages and salary, power and fuel |\n| Uncontrollable | Cannot be controlled even with appropriate managerial influence | Rates and taxes, depreciation, interest on borrowings |\n\n### Why split fixed from variable\n\nThe primary objective of splitting semi-variable expenses into fixed and variable is to ascertain **marginal costs**. It also helps in (SM 4.6–4.7):\n\n1. **Controlling expenses**: fixed costs are policy costs, hard to reduce; variable costs are controlled by allowances related to the output achieved.\n2. **Preparation of budget estimates**: the flexible budget estimates costs at each level of activity (worked in Example 2 below).\n3. **Decision making**: pricing in a recession or for export, make or buy, shut down or continue. Marginal costing, CVP and break-even analysis all rest on this split ([[cma.mc.basics|marginal cost and contribution]]).\n\n### Four steps, five stages, from estimate to unit\n\nActual overheads are known only after the accounts close, but cost sheets are needed now. So overheads are estimated in advance and charged to products at **pre-determined rates**, and the difference is adjusted at year end (SM 4.7–4.8).\n\nThe Study Material tells this journey twice. Its procedure for manufacturing overheads has **four steps**: estimation and collection, assignment, absorption, and treatment of under- or over-absorption (SM 4.8–4.10). Its chart of the steps for the distribution of overheads has **five stages**: estimation, allocation, apportionment, re-apportionment and absorption (SM 4.11). Same road: the chart splits assignment into its three parts and stops before the year-end adjustment.\n\n::: diagram The journey of a factory overhead\n{\"type\": \"flow\", \"w\": 420, \"nodes\": [{\"id\": \"e\", \"kind\": \"start\", \"label\": \"1. Estimation\\nand collection\"}, {\"id\": \"al\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"2a. Allocation:\\nwhole items\"}, {\"id\": \"ap\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"2b. Apportionment:\\nshared items\"}, {\"id\": \"re\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"2c. Re-apportionment:\\nservice to production\"}, {\"id\": \"ab\", \"kind\": \"process\", \"label\": \"3. Absorption:\\ninto units\"}, {\"id\": \"uo\", \"kind\": \"end\", \"label\": \"4. Under- or\\nover-absorption\"}], \"edges\": [[\"e\", \"al\"], [\"al\", \"ap\"], [\"ap\", \"re\"], [\"re\", \"ab\"], [\"ab\", \"uo\"]], \"caption\": \"Allocation takes whole items to one department, apportionment splits shared items on a fair basis, re-apportionment moves service departments' totals to production departments, and absorption charges them to units at a pre-determined rate. Stages 2a and 2b together are the primary distribution; 2c is the secondary distribution. ICAI groups 2a to 2c as assignment.\"}\n:::\n\n::: steps\n1. **Estimation and collection.** Estimate overheads from past figures adjusted for known future changes. Sources: (a) invoices, (b) stores requisitions, (c) the wage analysis book, (d) journal entries (SM 4.8). Collection is usually through **standing orders**: a sanction for indirect expenses under various heads, each head given a number that continues from year to year (SM 4.11–4.12).\n2. **Assignment.** The guiding principle is the traceability of the overhead in an economically feasible manner, on one of two principles: **cause and effect** (the cause is the process, operation or activity, the effect is the incurrence of cost) or **benefit received** (overheads apportioned in proportion to the benefit each cost object receives) (SM 4.8). Assignment has three parts: (a) **allocation**, (b) **apportionment**, (c) **re-apportionment** ([[cma.oh.primary|primary distribution]] does the first two, [[cma.oh.secondary|secondary distribution]] the third).\n3. **Absorption.** Each production department's overheads are recovered from its output. Variable manufacturing overheads are absorbed on actual production; fixed manufacturing overheads on ==normal capacity== (SM 4.10). Rates and methods: [[cma.oh.absorb|absorption rates and the machine hour rate]].\n4. **Under- or over-absorption.** There is bound to be some difference between the actual and the absorbed overheads; it is adjusted keeping in view its reasons (SM 4.10) ([[cma.oh.underover|under- and over-absorption]]).\n:::\n\nThe whole exercise serves two purposes: (a) to charge products with an **equitable portion** of factory overheads, and (b) to charge them **immediately** as a job is completed, without waiting for actual figures (SM 4.10).\n\n### Departments first: departmentalisation\n\nA factory has **production departments**, which make the product, and **service departments**, which do not take part in production directly but provide auxiliary services to other cost centres: engineering, quality control, laboratory, canteen, stores, time office, dispensary (SM 4.9). Collecting overheads department by department, or by smaller **cost centres** (machine or personnel), is **departmentalisation** ([[cma.intro.terms|cost centres]]). Its advantages (SM 4.12–4.13):\n\n1. **Better estimation of expenses**: departmental expenses can be estimated almost exactly.\n2. **Better control**: you can see which department overspent.\n3. **Ascertainment of cost for each department**: a job is charged only for the departments it passes through.\n4. **Suitable method of costing**: e.g. batch costing in a parts department, single output costing in assembly.\n\n## Formats and formulas\n\n*Write the definitions in ICAI's words; they are asked as MCQs and as \"distinguish\" questions.*\n\n::: definition Overheads\nOverheads represent expenses that have been incurred in providing certain ancillary facilities or services which facilitate or make possible the carrying out of the production process; by themselves these services are not of any use. (SM 4.63)\n:::\n\n::: definition Cost allocation\nThe term \"allocation\" refers to assignment or allotment of an entire item of cost to a particular cost center or cost unit. (SM 4.63)\n:::\n\n::: definition Cost apportionment\nApportionment implies \"the allotment of proportions of items of cost to cost centres or departments\". (SM 4.9)\n:::\n\n::: definition Re-apportionment\nThe process of assigning service department overheads to production departments is called reassignment or re-apportionment. (SM 4.9)\n:::\n\n::: definition Absorption\nThe process of recovering overheads of a department or any other cost center from its output is called recovery or absorption. (SM 4.10)\n:::\n\n::: formula Budgeted overhead at a new output\n$$\\text{Budget} = F + SF + (V + SV) \\times \\frac{Q_1}{Q_0}$$\nF = fixed overheads; SF = fixed part of the semi-variable overheads; V = variable overheads and SV = variable part of the semi-variable overheads, both at the old output; $Q_0$ = old output, $Q_1$ = new output. Split the semi-variable cost first; only the variable parts move with output (SM 4.6–4.7). Splitting methods are in [[cma.intro.behaviour|cost behaviour]].\n:::\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Kesar's \"miscellaneous\" ledger\nThe samosa division's \"miscellaneous\" head for March holds these items:\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Rent of the factory building | 1,20,000 |\n| Lubricants and cotton waste for the fryers | 8,000 |\n| Salary of the factory supervisor | 45,000 |\n| Printed pouches in which each pack is sealed | 64,000 |\n| Corrugated cartons for dispatch to distributors | 38,000 |\n| Salaries of head-office accounts staff | 90,000 |\n| Audit fees | 25,000 |\n| Salesmen's commission | 52,000 |\n| Instagram campaign for the peri-peri samosa | 75,000 |\n| Delivery van diesel and driver | 41,000 |\n| Transit insurance | 6,000 |\n| Cold storage rent at the city warehouse | 30,000 |\n| Depreciation of the office building | 18,000 |\n| Directors' remuneration | 1,00,000 |\n| Diwali gift tins designed to attract buyers | 22,000 |\n| Rent of the sales office | 20,000 |\n\n**Required:** Calculate the (i) production, (ii) administration, (iii) selling and (iv) distribution costs.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Production cost**\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Rent of the factory building | 1,20,000 |\n| Lubricants and cotton waste (indirect material) | 8,000 |\n| Salary of the factory supervisor | 45,000 |\n| Printed pouches (primary packing) | 64,000 |\n| **Total** | **2,37,000** |\n\n**(ii) Administration cost**\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Salaries of head-office accounts staff | 90,000 |\n| Audit fees | 25,000 |\n| Depreciation of the office building | 18,000 |\n| Directors' remuneration | 1,00,000 |\n| **Total** | **2,33,000** |\n\n**(iii) Selling cost**\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Salesmen's commission | 52,000 |\n| Instagram campaign (advertisement) | 75,000 |\n| Diwali gift tins (fancy packing) | 22,000 |\n| Rent of the sales office | 20,000 |\n| **Total** | **1,69,000** |\n\n**(iv) Distribution cost**\n\n| Item | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Corrugated cartons for dispatch (secondary packing) | 38,000 |\n| Delivery van diesel and driver | 41,000 |\n| Transit insurance | 6,000 |\n| Cold storage rent at the city warehouse | 30,000 |\n| **Total** | **1,15,000** |\n\nCheck: 2,37,000 + 2,33,000 + 1,69,000 + 1,15,000 = ₹7,54,000, the whole ledger.\n\nNotes on the three packing items (SM 4.60): primary packing, which protects the product or makes it convenient to handle, is part of production cost; packing to carry the product from factory to customer is distribution cost; fancy packing to attract customers is advertising, so a selling overhead.\n:::\n::::\n\n::::: example Kesar budgets April by \"just adding 25%\"\nIn March the samosa factory packed 80,000 packs, and its overheads were:\n\n| Overhead | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---:|\n| Fixed | 6,00,000 |\n| Variable | 4,80,000 |\n| Semi-variable (30% fixed) | 3,00,000 |\n| **Total** | **13,80,000** |\n\nApril's output is expected to be 1,00,000 packs, 25% more, so Kesar's budget simply adds 25% to everything: ₹13,80,000 × 1,00,000 ÷ 80,000 = ₹17,25,000.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Prepare the overhead budget for April.\n\n(ii) Calculate the overhead per pack in March and in April, and comment on Kesar's figure.\n\n:::: answer Show the working\n**Working note: split the semi-variable cost.** Fixed part = 30% × ₹3,00,000 = ₹90,000; variable part = ₹2,10,000.\n\n**(i) Overhead budget for April (1,00,000 packs)**\n\n| Overhead | Working | Amount (₹) |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Fixed | unchanged | 6,00,000 |\n| Variable | 4,80,000 × 1,00,000 ÷ 80,000 | 6,00,000 |\n| Semi-variable, fixed part | unchanged | 90,000 |\n| Semi-variable, variable part | 2,10,000 × 1,00,000 ÷ 80,000 | 2,62,500 |\n| **Total** | | **15,52,500** |\n\n**(ii) Overhead per pack**: March ₹13,80,000 ÷ 80,000 = **₹17.25**; April ₹15,52,500 ÷ 1,00,000 = **₹15.53** (15.525).\n\n**Comment.** Only the variable parts grow with output; the ₹6,90,000 of fixed overhead is spread over more packs, so the cost per pack falls. Kesar's ₹17,25,000 treats every rupee as variable and overstates the budget by ₹1,72,500: the \"wrong budgeting\" the Study Material warns about (SM 4.7).\n\n::: diagram April's budget: two lines from the same March point\n{\"type\": \"plot\", \"h\": 300, \"x\": [0, 120000], \"y\": [0, 2100000], \"legend\": \"outside\",\n \"xlabel\": \"packs a month\", \"ylabel\": \"overheads (₹)\",\n \"xticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [40000, \"40,000\"], [80000, \"80,000\"], [100000, \"1,00,000\"], [120000, \"1,20,000\"]],\n \"yticks\": [[0, \"0\"], [690000, \"6,90,000\"], [1380000, \"13,80,000\"], [1725000, \"17,25,000\"]],\n \"series\": [\n  {\"label\": \"correct: 6,90,000 + ₹8.625 a pack\", \"fn\": \"690000+8.625*x\", \"color\": \"a\"},\n  {\"label\": \"Kesar: ₹17.25 a pack\", \"fn\": \"17.25*x\", \"color\": \"d\", \"dash\": true},\n  {\"pts\": [[100000, 0], [100000, 1725000]], \"color\": \"mute\", \"dash\": true, \"legend\": false}\n ],\n \"points\": [{\"x\": 80000, \"y\": 1380000, \"label\": \"March\", \"pos\": \"left\"}, {\"x\": 100000, \"y\": 1552500, \"label\": \"15,52,500\", \"pos\": \"right\"}, {\"x\": 100000, \"y\": 1725000, \"label\": \"17,25,000\", \"pos\": \"left\"}],\n \"caption\": \"Blue: the budget line with the fixed ₹6,90,000 kept fixed; orange dashed: Kesar's line through the origin. They meet at March's 80,000 packs and part at April's 1,00,000.\"}\n:::\n::::\n:::::\n\n:::: example Allocate or apportion? Eight items in the samosa factory\nThe factory has two production departments, Frying and Packing, and two service departments, Stores and Maintenance. For each item, state whether it is allocated or apportioned, and to which department or on what kind of basis.\n\n1. Gas for the fryers, read from Frying's own gas meter.\n2. Salary of the works manager, who runs the whole factory.\n3. Depreciation of the pouch-sealing machine installed in Packing.\n4. Rent of the factory building, which houses all four departments.\n5. Wages of the storekeepers.\n6. Electricity for lighting, on one meter for the whole factory.\n7. Electricity for lighting the Packing hall, which has its own sub-meter.\n8. Subsidy to the canteen used by workers of all four departments.\n\n::: answer Model answer\n| Item | Treatment | Department or basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Gas, own meter | Allocated | Frying |\n| 2. Works manager's salary | Apportioned | Technical estimate of time (managerial salaries, SM 4.14) |\n| 3. Depreciation of the sealing machine | Allocated | Packing |\n| 4. Factory rent | Apportioned | Floor area (or volume) |\n| 5. Storekeepers' wages | Allocated | Stores, a service department |\n| 6. Lighting, common meter | Apportioned | Light points, area or metered units |\n| 7. Lighting, Packing's sub-meter | Allocated | Packing |\n| 8. Canteen subsidy | Apportioned | Number of workers |\n\nItems 6 and 7 are the same kind of expense with opposite treatment: as the Study Material puts it, the allocation or apportionment of an expense is not dependent on its nature, but on the relationship between the expense and the cost centre (SM 4.16). The bases are chosen in [[cma.oh.primary|primary distribution]].\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### Fine print: packing, canteens, welfare and training\n\nThe Study Material's \"treatment of certain items\" settles several classification questions (SM 4.60–4.62):\n\n| Item | Treatment |\n|---|---|\n| Primary packing (protects the product, eases handling) | Production cost |\n| Packing for transport to the customer | Distribution cost |\n| Special packing at the customer's request | Charged to that work order or job |\n| Fancy packing to attract customers | Selling overhead (advertising) |\n| Fringe benefits (overtime, shift allowance, holiday pay, pension) | Production overhead; if large, a supplementary wage rate |\n| Removal and re-erection of machines | Production overhead; Costing P&L if due to faulty planning |\n| Canteen subsidy | Production overhead, by number of workers; a share to office overhead if office staff use it |\n| Welfare activities | General overhead, split between factory, office, selling and distribution by number of persons |\n| Training of factory workers | Cost of production; office or sales staff training to those overheads |\n\nAdministration, selling, distribution and R&D overheads have their own lesson: [[cma.oh.ads|administration, selling, distribution and R&D overheads]].\n\n### Allocation is \"the wider term\"\n\nThe Study Material closes its comparison with two points: the relationship between the expense and the cost centre decides whether it is allocated or apportioned, and \"allocation is a much wider term than apportionment\" (SM 4.16). Its summary defines allocation as the allotment of an entire item to a cost centre **or a cost unit**, not only to departments.\n\n### Controllable for whom?\n\n\"Uncontrollable\" is always relative to a person. The tool room's expenditure is controllable by its foreman, but the share of it apportioned to the machine shop is not controllable by the machine-shop foreman (SM 1.33). In fact, says the Study Material, no cost is uncontrollable; it is only in relation to a particular individual that a cost is controllable or not.\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam How ICAI asks the classification and the stages\nIn the documents read for this lesson:\n\n- **MCQs**: the chapter's own Test Your Knowledge asks which term means allotment of whole items of cost (cost allocation), where primary packing belongs (production cost), what directors' remuneration is (administration overhead) and which is not a functional class (fixed overhead) (SM 4.65).\n- **Classify a list, 6 marks**: MTP January 2025 Series II Q3(b) gave twelve items and asked for administration, selling and distribution cost separately; the answer is three short tables, one per function.\n- **Theory**: \"Distinguish between cost allocation and cost absorption\" (RTP November 2023, old scheme, Q14(e); the chapter's question 4); \"Discuss the difference between allocation and apportionment\" (the chapter's question 7); controllable versus uncontrollable costs (MTP May 2024 Series I Q6(c), 4 marks).\n- In cost sheets, primary packing was added to arrive at the cost of production (MTP January 2025 Series I, Q3(a)).\n:::\n\n::: exam Traps\n- A cost with its own meter, its own machine or its own staff is allocated, whatever its nature.\n- Secondary packing is distribution; fancy packing is selling; primary packing is production.\n- A service department's own wages (storekeepers, maintenance fitters) are allocated to that service department, never treated as direct wages of a product.\n:::\n\n::: trick A-for-All, P-for-Part\n**A**llocation takes **a**ll of one item to one centre; a**P**portionment takes **p**arts of one item to several centres. Works whenever the question gives a single expense; for a list, decide item by item.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n**Distinguish between allocation and apportionment** (SM 4.16): write it as a table, then the two points below it.\n\n| Point | Allocation | Apportionment |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is charged | Whole items of cost identifiable with one department (e.g. indirect wages of each department obtained separately) | Proportions of an item of cost (e.g. the cost of a service shared by the departments that avail it) |\n| Nature of the process | A direct process of charging expenses to cost centres | An indirect process: the appropriate portion borne by each department must be identified |\n\n- The relationship between the expense and the cost centre, not its nature, decides whether an expense is allocated or apportioned.\n- Allocation is a much wider term than apportionment.\n\n**Distinguish between cost allocation and cost absorption**: allocation is the allotment of a whole item of cost to a cost centre or cost unit; absorption is the process of absorbing all overheads allocated to or apportioned over a cost centre or production department into the units produced. Allocation gets costs **into** cost centres; absorption gets them **out**, into products.\n\n**Advantages of departmentalisation** (four points, SM 4.12–4.13) and **advantages of separating fixed and variable overheads** (the objective plus three points, SM 4.6–4.7) are written as numbered points with one line each, in ICAI's order.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: \"power is always apportioned\"\nStudents learn \"power: horse power × machine hours\" and apply it even when a department has its own meter. **Fix:** check the facts first; a metered or exclusively used cost is allocated in full, and only the shared remainder is apportioned.\n:::\n\n::: mistake The 25% that ate the budget\nIncreasing every overhead in proportion to output, as Kesar did, overstated April by ₹1,72,500. **Fix:** split semi-variable costs, keep the fixed parts fixed, and move only the variable parts.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Cartons in the factory, pouches on the truck\nPrimary and secondary packing swapped. **Fix:** ask what the packing does. Protects or holds the product: production. Carries it to the customer: distribution. Attracts the customer: selling.\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: Which of these classifies overheads by their behaviour (nature)?\n- [ ] Factory, administration, selling and distribution\n- [x] Fixed, variable and semi-variable\n- [ ] Indirect material, indirect employee cost and indirect expenses\n- [ ] Controllable and uncontrollable\n> Behaviour means how a cost responds to output: fixed, variable, semi-variable. The first option is by function, the third by element, the fourth by control.\n\nQ: The Pune café's electricity was ₹50,000 last month for 20,000 cups, 40% of it fixed. What is the budgeted electricity for 25,000 cups (₹)?\n= 57500\n> Fixed ₹20,000 stays; variable ₹30,000 × 25,000 ÷ 20,000 = ₹37,500. Total ₹57,500. Scaling the whole ₹50,000 by 25% gives ₹62,500.\n\nQ: Case scenario. Tapri's cold-chain hub has three departments under one roof. The walk-in cold room in the Dairy section has its own electricity sub-meter, which read ₹18,000 for May; the rest of the hub's electricity, ₹42,000, is on the main meter. Ritu must charge Dairy for electricity. Which treatment is correct?\n- [ ] Apportion ₹60,000 among the three departments by floor area\n- [x] Allocate ₹18,000 to Dairy and apportion ₹42,000 among the three departments\n- [ ] Allocate ₹60,000 to Dairy, the biggest user\n- [ ] Apportion ₹18,000 among the three departments by horse power\n> A sub-metered cost belongs wholly to one cost centre, so it is allocated; only the common-meter amount is shared. The relationship with the cost centre, not the nature of electricity, decides.\n\nQ: Which of these are uncontrollable overheads in ICAI's examples? Select all that apply.\n- [x] Rates and taxes\n- [ ] Power and fuel\n- [x] Interest on borrowings\n- [x] Depreciation\n> ICAI lists rates and taxes, depreciation and interest on borrowings as uncontrollable, and materials, wages and salary, and power and fuel as controllable (SM 4.5).\n\nQ: In April Tapri's premix plant spent ₹40,000 on the sealed pouches each pack is filled into, ₹25,000 on gift sleeves designed to attract buyers, ₹30,000 on corrugated cartons to carry packs to distributors, and ₹12,000 on wooden crates that an export customer asked for on its order. How much of this is distribution overhead (₹)?\n= 30000\n> Only the transport cartons are distribution overhead (₹30,000). The pouches are primary packing, part of production cost; the gift sleeves are fancy packing, a selling (advertising) overhead; special packing at a customer's request is charged to that customer's order (SM 4.60).\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Overheads: costs not conveniently traceable to a cost unit; the aggregate of indirect material, indirect employee cost and indirect expenses.\n- Four classifications, in ICAI's order: function (factory, administration, selling and distribution), nature (fixed, variable, semi-variable), element, control.\n- Splitting fixed and variable serves marginal costing, then control, budget estimates and decisions; budget only the variable parts in proportion to output.\n- Stages: estimation and collection (standing orders) → allocation → apportionment → re-apportionment → absorption → under- or over-absorption.\n- Allocation: whole item to one cost centre; apportionment: proportions of an item on a fair basis (cause and effect or benefit received).\n- The relationship with the cost centre decides allocation or apportionment; allocation is the wider term.\n- Variable manufacturing overheads are absorbed on actual production, fixed ones on normal capacity.\n- Primary packing: production; secondary: distribution; fancy: selling. (\"Miscellaneous\" is not a function.)"});
